ht  but i think the part of this theory that  still is very relevant is how we  interact with objects that we deem to  have an aura when an object is deemed to  have an intrinsic value  we might guard it or hide it away  and we probably spend more time looking  at it  in a more contemplative manner when  people say when you go look at the mona  lisa try to experience the painting and  try to be in its presence  but with reproducible works of art we  tend to look at it less carefully or  speed through it in a less focused  manner it's the same with information  when books were handwritten over time  span of ears they used to hide them from  the public because they were scared that  they were gonna get damaged or burned or  lost and they would literally chain the  books to the desks and what happened is  when you finally got a hold of the book  you would read it and really taken every  word of the book but now with massive  amounts of information everywhere we go  i think it's pretty safe to say that we  take things a little less to heart  umberto echo author and semiotician said  that books in his library had a aura he  had a collection of 50 000 books and he  said as he would roam around his library  on more than one occasion he would  stumble across a book and when he read  it that book magically contained  information that was vital to his  research he was a really strange person  but he called his library an  anti-library and he filled it with books  that he'd never read  he said lots of people have a library to  show off their knowledge to say look at  all these books i read but he uses it as  a research tool and he roams around his  ivory and i think he believes that he is  physically inches away from discovering  new information  and that physical presence of the books  had the power to pull him closer to what  he needed this idea of aura or the loss  of aura in our culture isn't just  happening with art or information but  it's also happening with our built  environments  cities are made up of streets and  streets are characterized by the  building's facades a building's facade  is basically a fancy name well for the  building's face  it expresses the building's character  and it gives you a glimpse of what might  be happening inside  and it's designed to catch people's  attention with our culture's obsession  with images  we experience architecture differently  and our environments are adapting to  satisfy this new way of experiencing  architecture  all the way down to the process of how  we design we tend to focus more on the  shareable moments in our buildings and i  am totally guilty of this architecture  is becoming increasingly rushed and  impatient  leading to issues of experiential  impoverishment our built environments  are void of any sense of mystery or aura  and just like what's happened with art  or information  i think the way we experience the world  is becoming more and more  one-dimensional  and we start to value architecture not  necessarily for its experiential  qualities but for its ability to be  photographed and seen by the largest  number of people  [Music]  venice is probably one of the most  extreme examples of a city whose aura  has been completely and utterly  destroyed thanks to the iconic images  it's become one of the most popular  tourist destinations in the world it  gets something around 20 million  visitors every year  it shows up in movies and books and it's  probably one of the most universally  recognizable locations i'm not gonna lie  when i first visited venice i also have  my preconceptions until i learned this  concept called derive which directly  translates into drifting it's actually a  method devised by the situationist  internationals for navigating through  venice and they define derive as an  unplanned unexpected passage through an  urban landscape where participants stop  their daily routines and allow  themselves to be drawn by the  attractions of the terrain and the  encounters that they find there it's a  place of both connection and  non-connection  a place designed specifically for  getting lost from a totally functional  point of view venice is kind of the  worst place to live  it floods it's full of tourists the  buildings are old it's practically not  accessible for anyone with mobility  issues and you can't drive you can't  bike and in that sense it's the ultimate  anti-city so why is it that so many  venetians who's been driven out of the  city for all of these reasons that i  just mentioned  still dream about one day moving back  there and living there i think it  ultimately has something to do with aura  [Music]  that in the end it's a totally and  completely unique place there's no other  place like it on earth  there's no one else that can replicate  the environment the history  and the city's presence in time and  space not even the most perfect replica  has the aura of venice but as an  architect i still try to look for  patterns in the built environment not  necessarily to replicate but to see if  there's any elements that we can learn  from and as i try to study the build  fabric to understand why it feels so  different from other cities i was able  to distill certain elements or recurring  conditions that helped in creating this  sense of mystery framing  framing is used to hide or highlight  certain elements in this case a narrow  gap between the corners of two buildings  perfectly frame the corner of the  basilica de ferrari dead ends  a key aspect of the reef or drifting is  allowing yourself to be drawn by the  attractions of the terrain and as i  drift through these alleys i kept coming  across these dead ends initially i'd  turn around and backtrack but on  instances when i kept going i found that  it's not a dead end after all and the  corner reveals another alley point of  interest  as i moved through these narrow alleys i  kept feeling a constant pulling like a  push to keep walking  it's obviously the narrowness of the  tunnel but also the light at the end of  the alley or a tunnel that created this  point of interest  compression and release when i followed  the light and reached the end of the  tunnel i would often be pleasantly  surprised to find myself in a  light-filled courtyard or an opening to  a canal or anything that created a  feeling of expansion and as i walk for  hours and hours i felt a constant  compression and release in the landscape  layering layering creates anticipation  depth and a more interesting and  memorable narrative when walking through  space  well all of these conditions did for me  is it created a sense of curiosity that  made me want to discover more and also a  kind of safety i've never  once felt scared or like i was in danger  the city is so so safe because there's  no dangerous corners that sounds like a  really obvious statement but let's say a  typical city with streets and back  alleys the alleys are the leftover  spaces and you know that's where all the  dodgy stuff happens  but in venice everything is an alley and  there's no front and back and everything  is just a continuous labyrinth and  there's doors and windows onto each path  so after walking around for a while and  just being left by my curiosity that's  when i discovered really cool gems lots  of people think venice is a static city  i mean there's been virtually no new  construction in venice for hundreds of  years but actually it has been  transforming at a micro scale since we  can only guess the functions inside the  buildings urge us to discover more  rather than telling us exactly what they  are you see this especially in the  neighborhood of canary  which was once a jewish ghetto these  seemingly benign residential buildings  when you go inside they have synagogues  with ornate lavish details that you can  only discover by exploring further  inside  it's our knowledge the things that we're  sure of that makes the world go wrong  and keeps us from seeing and 